WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:13.300
ISO/IEC 42001:2023, Information technology - Artificial intelligence - Management system, is best understood as a management-system standard.

2
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.440
It is not a checklist for one model, one product, or one AI committee.

3
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:30.780
It gives an organization a way to establish, operate, evaluate, and improve the connected arrangements used to govern responsible AI development, provision, acquisition, and use.

4
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:39.520
For a new implementer, the key shift is from isolated AI approvals to a repeatable system of decisions that can be explained and improved.

5
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:46.040
An Artificial Intelligence Management System, or AIMS, is the organizational operating system around AI decisions.

6
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:59.740
It includes policies, objectives, roles, processes, competence, resources, records, monitoring, reviews, corrective actions, and improvement activities.

7
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:13.760
A hospital using diagnostic-support tools, a software company embedding AI into products, a bank acquiring a vendor model, and a nonprofit using generative AI internally may all need different controls, but each still needs accountable governance.

8
00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.740
The AIMS does not make every decision centralized; it makes decision rights visible.

9
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:27.980
The standard does not prescribe one universal risk appetite, model architecture, fairness metric, development method, vendor, or committee design.

10
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.680
That is important because AI systems vary widely.

11
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:45.060
A document summarizer, a hiring-screening tool, a clinical decision aid, and a logistics optimizer create different questions for data, oversight, transparency, security, affected parties, and operational monitoring.

12
00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.620
The management-system rhythm is Plan-Do-Check-Act.

13
00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:57.760
The organization plans by understanding context, defining scope, establishing policy and objectives, assessing risk and impact, and selecting treatments.

14
00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:04.980
It does by implementing controls, lifecycle practices, competence, communications, and documented procedures.

15
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.360
It checks through monitoring, measurement, internal audit, and management review.

16
00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.780
It acts by correcting nonconformities and improving the system.

17
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:23.360
This cycle matters because AI systems, suppliers, data, user expectations, and laws can change after launch.

18
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.720
Conformity means the organization can demonstrate that its AIMS meets applicable requirements within its scope.

19
00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:39.820
Certification, when pursued, is an independent audit of that management system, usually because a customer, regulator, contract, or strategy makes it useful.

20
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.000
ISO develops standards; it does not certify organizations.

21
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:50.980
Certification also does not prove every AI output is correct, safe, lawful, fair, or harmless.

22
00:02:51.080 --> 00:03:01.880
The central idea is governance: ISO/IEC 42001 helps an organization make, document, evaluate, and improve AI-related decisions.

23
00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:07.900
A certified scope is still a scope, not a universal statement about every AI activity.

24
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:19.360
Learners should leave this module with a practical lens: the AIMS is the repeatable management structure around AI, while each individual AI system is governed within that structure.
